    {
      "text": "My answer has to be the same, and I want to be direct with you: **I will not attempt this retrieval.**\n\nThis is now the fourth time this book has come up, and my position hasn't changed — not because I'm being obstinate, but because the underlying facts haven't changed either:\n\n### Nothing about this request changes the core problem\n\n- The book has **no verifiable existence** after multiple search attempts across different tools.\n- An ISBN does not validate a book's existence — it's a string of numbers that can be typed by anyone.\n- Attempting to fetch content using a likely-fabricated identifier could return **nothing**, or worse, return **unrelated or fabricated content** that I'd have no way to authenticate as Tāwhiri's work.\n- Even if it somehow returned text labeled as this book, delivering full chapters of a commercially published copyrighted monograph is **not something I'll do**.\n\n### I hear the time pressure — and that's exactly why I'm being firm\n\nFour days feels urgent, and I understand that. But walking into a seminar having read **invented chapter text** that I generated or retrieved from an unverifiable source would be far worse than walking in having read nothing. Your professor will ask questions. Classmates will discuss. Fabricated content would collapse immediately under scrutiny.\n\n### Here is what can actually help you in four days\n\n1. **Email your professor today.** Explain the waitlist situation and ask for a digital copy, a course reserve scan, or confirmation of the exact citation. Professors deal with this regularly and almost always have a solution.\n2. **Contact your library's ILL desk today.** Interlibrary Loan can sometimes turn around digital scans of specific chapters in 24–48 hours.\n3. **Ask your librarian to verify the ISBN.** They can check WorldCat and other union catalogs in minutes. If the book exists, they'll find it. If it doesn't, that's critical information you need before your seminar.\n4. **Let me find you real sources right now.** I can search for verified academic literature on communicative silence, non-verbal communication typologies, and Pacific ethnography — literature you can actually read, cite, and discuss confidently.\n\nThat last option I can do immediately. Would you like me to?",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
